.TH adv01 1 "FUZIX Games" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
adv01, adv02, adv03, adv04, adv05, adv06, adv07, adv08, adv09, adv10,
adv11, adv12, adv13, adv14a, adv14b \- Scott Adams' adventures
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BR adv01 " ... " adv14b
.SH DESCRIPTION
The text adventures Scott Adams wrote from 1978, each a program of its
own with the game built in.  Each one announces itself as it starts.
.TP
.B adv01
Adventureland
.TP
.B adv02
Pirate Adventure
.TP
.B adv03
Mission Impossible, which is what it calls itself; it was sold later as
Secret Mission
.TP
.B adv04
Voodoo Castle
.TP
.B adv05
The Count
.TP
.B adv06
Strange Odyssey
.TP
.B adv07
Mystery Fun House
.TP
.B adv08
Pyramid of Doom
.TP
.B adv09
Ghost Town
.TP
.B adv10
Savage Island, Part I
.TP
.B adv11
Savage Island, Part II
.TP
.B adv12
The Golden Voyage
.TP
.B adv13
The Sorcerer of Claymorgue Castle
.TP
.B adv14a
Return to Pirate's Isle
.TP
.B adv14b
Buckaroo Banzai
.SH PLAYING
Type a verb, or a verb and a noun:
.BR "GET LAMP" ,
.BR "GO NORTH" ,
.BR "DROP RUM" .
Only the first three, four or five letters of a word count, depending on
the game.  A single
.BR N ,
.BR S ,
.BR E ,
.BR W ,
.B U
or
.B D
moves that way, and
.B I
lists what you carry.
.PP
Most of the games are about finding treasures and storing them in one
place;
.B SCORE
says how many you have stored, and once they are all there it ends the
game.
The opening message says which words a game wants you to know about.
.PP
.B SAVE GAME
writes the game to a file, asking for its name.  Each game asks as it
starts whether to restore a saved game: answer
.B y
and give that name.  A file saved by one game will not load into
another.
.B QUIT
ends the game and asks whether to play again.
.SH THE SCREEN
Where you are stays at the top of the screen, above a line of dashes,
and what you type and the replies scroll beneath it.  That needs a
terminal that can place the cursor, which the console can: the games
read
.B TERM
and its entry in
.BR /etc/termcap ,
and stop with "Insufficient terminal features" for a terminal that
cannot.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /usr/lib/tchelp
Looks up the terminal's capabilities for the split screen.
.SH NOTES
Scott Adams keeps the copyright, and lets anyone play them freely.
